Quantum dots are semiconductor nanocrystals composed of just hundreds of atoms that, despite being invisible to the naked eye, shine brilliantly by emitting visible light whose color depends precisely on their size: larger dots confine longer wavelengths such as red, while smaller dots emit shorter, higher-energy wavelengths like blue. Governed by quantum mechanics, they display phenomena like wave\'96particle duality and quantum confinement, but one of their most fascinating behaviors is their ability to undergo F\'f6rster Resonance Energy Transfer (FRET)\'97a non-radiative process in which one quantum dot (the donor) transfers energy to another (the acceptor) when three conditions are met: the donor\'92s emission spectrum overlaps with the acceptor\'92s absorption spectrum, the two are within a few nanometers of each other, and the donor\'96acceptor relationship is energetically compatible. Beyond its scientific importance, this interplay of proximity, resonance, and alignment provides a compelling metaphor for human relationships: like FRET, meaningful connections emerge when individuals are close enough, attuned in energy, and aligned in resonance\'97conditions under which something luminous and transformative can occur.\
